Well, but that _is_ the answer -- she's letting her
XL outrun her AC improvement, probably 1) by killing
stuff her pets should be killing, 2) by killing
stuff she could out-run instead, and 3) by skipping
the chance to try every uncursed piece of armor in
upper mines dropped by monsters her pets kill. It's
not very hard to hit Mines Town at XL 1 and AC -5 or
so with the opposite set of behaviors, and find it
full of really wimpy monsters your pets can easily
handle for you.

>> In any case, I've been wondering if I'm better
>> off dumping the starting quarterstaff for
>> something else.  I keep trying to get levels in
>> daggers, but usually that means my character
>> dies before the daggers get leveled up.  But
>> even so, daggers are primarily a ranged weapon.
>> Is there anything better than that quarterstaff
>> for hand-to-hand?  Of things that are commonly
>> found, that is?

And here again the problem is highlighted; if you're
worrying about what would be a good melee weapon for
your wizard, the implication is that you're letting
your wizard engage in melee -- don't do that, it
keeps getting you killed.  Think back to AD&D
adventurer parties -- the wizard always stands way
in the back, casting spells, with the other PCs
forming the front line. In NetHack, there _are_ no
"other PCs", so either let your pets fill that role,
or run like hell when they can't or won't.

> I've got a wizard on the ascension run right now.
> I think I ascend close to half my wizards. Daggers
> are the only way to go. That +2 "to Hit" in the
> early game is huge. I may keep my quarterstaff for
> a while, but I never spend any skill slots on it.
> Don't neglect your spells, the more you use them
> the sooner you can advance the skill and do better
> with them.

>> For example, how does a quarterstaff compare to
>> a short sword, assuming both of them are at the
>> same skill level?  And how do they compare when
>> they're maxed out in skill?

> A short sword is 3.5/4.5 and the quarterstaff is
> 3.5/3.5, and you start out basic in quarterstaff
> which is significantly better than unskilled. I
> don't think it wise to spend many skill slots on
> weapons as a wizard. I spend 6 on daggers and the
> rest on magic.

Sting is available to any character, and suffices
for wizards who have yet to acquire MagicBane. Using
a dagger as your melee weapon (as any class) lets
your skill slots do double duty and your skills
advance twice as fast: both melee and ranged use
count toward experience, and both melee and ranged
use advance with the same one skill enhancement.
It doesn't hurt either to do lots of enchanting of
daggers [though my own PCs find "enchant weapons"
scrolls only rarely; I've got an XL 10 thief just
now, 12,000 or so turns into the game, who has yet
to ID one, which with a mixed kit of five dozen
daggers ready for enchanting is really depressing].

> Use spells, daggers, your pet, Elbereth and
> prayer.

Heavy emphasis on "pets" there. The traditional
wizard's "familiar" translates exactly into the
NetHack "pet" -- no wizard should be without one,
and several is preferable.

